window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][27] = "<h1>Part V — Execution</h1>\n<span id=\"c15\"></span>\n<h2>C15 — Prioritization and Tradeoffs</h2>\n<span id=\"c15-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;You have five features and capacity for two. Which do you build?&quot; and &quot;The deadline just moved up by a month. What do you cut?&quot; are execution questions, and they are answered badly in a predictable way: the candidate names a framework (RICE, impact versus effort), assigns numbers with no source, and announces the top score as the answer. The interviewer is not testing whether you know acronyms. They are testing whether you can turn a goal into criteria, weigh options against those criteria with evidence, name what you give up, and defend a decision while staying open to changing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: &quot;which feature first,&quot; &quot;what would you cut,&quot; stakeholder-conflict and resource-conflict prompts at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often in execution rounds and often as a follow-up to a design or improvement answer (the interviewer asks, &quot;you proposed three things; which first?&quot;) <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the weekly work of a PM: a backlog that is always larger than capacity, and every choice a statement about what the team values.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether the decision follows from a stated goal <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for criteria derived from the goal, not borrowed from a template; for options sized with a reasoned estimate and an honest range; for tradeoffs named in terms of what is lost (not only &quot;pros and cons&quot;); for the riskiest assumption identified and tested early; for a decision, including what you will not do; and for a readiness to change the ranking when a better argument or new evidence arrives.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state the goal and the constraints (capacity, deadline, dependencies) before ranking anything.</li>\n<li>You derive the criteria from the goal and say how you weight them and why.</li>\n<li>You size the options with a chain of reasoning, and give a range or a sensitivity, not a single score.</li>\n<li>You name the tradeoffs and the opportunity cost of the choice, including the short-term against the long-term.</li>\n<li>You identify the riskiest assumption and propose the cheapest test, or a minimum viable product, before committing.</li>\n<li>You decide, say what you will not do, and say what would change your mind; and when you must refuse, you say no with a reason and an alternative.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end prioritization answers: a framework used as a decision machine (<a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>, C03), criteria that do not come from the goal, scores with invented precision, a list of pros and cons with no decision, building the whole thing when a test would do, and a &quot;no&quot; with no reason or alternative.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-1-prioritization-as-a-reasoned-decision\"></span>\n<h4>C15.1 Prioritization as a reasoned decision</h4>\n<p><strong>Prioritization</strong> is choosing what to do first under limited capacity, using stated criteria and acknowledged tradeoffs. Three things make it hard. There are always more ideas than capacity. The value of each idea is uncertain. And every choice has an <strong>opportunity cost</strong>: the value of the best alternative given up when a choice is made. Choosing feature A means not building B, and the true cost of A includes what B would have brought.</p>\n<p><strong>What is being decided.</strong> Four things, in order: the <em>goal</em> (what are we trying to achieve in this period?), the <em>criteria</em> (what makes an option good for that goal?), the <em>evidence</em> (what do we know about each option, and how sure are we?) and the <em>tradeoffs</em> (what do we give up?). A framework helps organize the last three and can never supply the first.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use a full prioritization process.</strong> If there is one clear option, just do it. If the decision is cheap and reversible (<a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, C02), decide quickly and learn: a long analysis costs more than a wrong choice. If the real problem is not capacity but alignment (people disagree on the goal), no scoring will help; resolve the goal first (C32). If the options are not comparable until you learn more, run a test first (C15.6).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked which of five features to build, a candidate says: &quot;I'd use RICE. Feature B scores 45,000, the highest, so build B.&quot; The interviewer asks what the team's goal is. The candidate has not said. If the goal is new-user activation, B (a feature mostly used by existing users) may be nearly irrelevant, and the score of 45,000 only shows that its reach is large. Worked example 1 shows the ranking changing when the same scoring is anchored to the goal. The framework produced a number; it did not make a decision.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>False precision:</em> a score like 45,000 looks exact and rests on guesses for reach, impact and confidence. <em>Gaming:</em> when scores decide, people inflate their favorite's confidence and shrink its effort; the process rewards persuasion and not truth. Both are why this chapter treats scoring as a way to organize thinking and test sensitivity.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-2-frameworks-when-each-works-and-when-it-fails\"></span>\n<h4>C15.2 Frameworks: when each works and when it fails</h4>\n<p>Several frameworks are in common use. FW-C15-02 is a chooser; FW-C15-03 covers RICE in detail. None is better in general; each fits some situations.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Framework</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Works when</th>\n<th>Fails when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RICE (reach × impact × confidence ÷ effort)</td>\n<td>Gives a comparable score per unit effort</td>\n<td>Many similar options for one goal, with data on reach</td>\n<td>Reach is not tied to the goal; impact is a guess; scores are gamed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ICE (impact, confidence, ease)</td>\n<td>A quick, lightweight score</td>\n<td>Fast triage of experiments or small ideas</td>\n<td>Used for big, strategic bets; no reach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value versus effort (2×2)</td>\n<td>Shows quick wins and expensive bets</td>\n<td>A workshop, to start a conversation</td>\n<td>Values and efforts are unsized; everything lands in &quot;quick win&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weighted scoring</td>\n<td>Scores options against weighted criteria</td>\n<td>Criteria differ from simple impact/effort (e.g., strategy fit, risk)</td>\n<td>Weights are arbitrary and tuned to a preferred result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MoSCoW (must, should, could, won't)</td>\n<td>Sorts scope for a fixed deadline</td>\n<td>Scoping a release; negotiating</td>\n<td>Everything is a &quot;must&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kano model</td>\n<td>Separates needs (must-haves), performance and delighters</td>\n<td>Understanding what customers expect versus value</td>\n<td>Used as a score; needs customer research</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost of delay (and its weighted form, WSJF)</td>\n<td>Ranks by value lost per unit of delay, per unit effort</td>\n<td>Time-sensitive items with different urgency profiles</td>\n<td>Delay costs cannot be estimated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opportunity scoring</td>\n<td>Importance versus satisfaction gaps from customers</td>\n<td>Customer-research-rich contexts</td>\n<td>No survey data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The rule.</strong> Use a framework to structure the discussion and expose assumptions, not to produce the answer. Whatever framework you use, three questions keep it honest: where did each number come from, what happens to the ranking if the least certain number changes (FW-C15-08), and does the top item serve the goal? A framework used without those questions is <a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A chooser prevents &quot;RICE for everything.&quot; <strong>When not to use any of them.</strong> When the decision is obvious from the goal and a single constraint, say so in a sentence.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-3-deriving-criteria-from-goals\"></span>\n<h4>C15.3 Deriving criteria from goals</h4>\n<p>Criteria that come from a template (&quot;impact, effort, confidence&quot;) are generic. Criteria derived from the goal are specific, and the specificity is what makes the decision defensible. FW-C15-04 is the ladder.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: the goal in measurable terms.</strong> &quot;Increase activation of new users from 28% to 35% this quarter&quot; (C05).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: what would make an option good for that goal?</strong> Ask: who must be affected (new users, not all users), by how much on the goal metric (impact on activation), how sure are we, how much capacity does it take, what else does it affect (risks, dependencies), and does it open later options? These become the criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: weights and a flip test.</strong> Weights say which criteria matter more <em>for this goal</em>. Test them: change the weights modestly and see whether the ranking changes. If the top option stays on top across reasonable weights, the choice is robust; if it flips, you have found the tradeoff that matters and should discuss it explicitly.</p>\n<p><strong>Example (invented).</strong> The goal is to shorten the time for new sellers on a marketplace to make their first sale. Criteria: impact on time to first sale (weight 40%), reach among new sellers (25%), confidence (15%), ease (20%). A guided listing wizard scores 4.45 out of 5, pricing suggestions 3.30, bulk upload 2.85 and an analytics dashboard 2.35. With the weights shifted to favor ease (impact 25%, reach 25%, confidence 15%, ease 35%), the wizard is still first at 4.15, and bulk upload (3.15) and pricing suggestions (3.00) swap places. The top choice is robust; the second place is not, and that is worth saying.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Criteria from the goal keep the ranking about what the team is trying to do. <strong>When not to build a scorecard.</strong> For two options, a short written comparison against the goal is clearer than a table.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-4-tradeoff-analysis-speed-quality-scope-and-short-term-a\"></span>\n<h4>C15.4 Tradeoff analysis: speed, quality, scope, and short term against long term</h4>\n<p>Every plan trades among scope (what is delivered), time (when), quality (how well) and resources (who and how many). FW-C15-05 is the triangle. Changing one moves the others, and the useful question is <em>which is fixed and which can flex?</em> A date fixed by a launch event and a quality bar fixed by safety leave scope as the lever; a scope fixed by a contract leaves time or resources.</p>\n<p>A <strong>time-box</strong> is a fixed period given to a piece of work, with scope that flexes to fit. It turns &quot;how long will this take?&quot; into &quot;what is the best we can do in two weeks?&quot;, which protects the date and forces choices about scope. <strong>Scope creep</strong> is the growth of a project's scope after it starts, without a matching change in time, resources or other scope, which delays or degrades delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Adding people.</strong> A common response to a tighter deadline is to add engineers. Example (invented): three engineers have 8 weeks, a capacity of 24 person-weeks, for 10 stories at about 2.4 person-weeks each. The deadline moves to 4 weeks. Capacity is 12 person-weeks, enough for 5 stories. Add two engineers: each is about 25% productive in week 1, 50% in week 2 and fully productive after, so each delivers 0.25 + 0.5 + 1 + 1 = 2.75 person-weeks in four weeks, 5.5 for both.</p>\n<p>But the three existing engineers lose about 20% of two weeks to onboarding: 3 × 2 × 0.2 = 1.2 person-weeks. Net gain 4.3, for 16.3 person-weeks, about 6.8 stories. Adding two people gets roughly seven stories, not ten, and the number of person-to-person communication paths grows from 3 (three people) to 10 (five people: 5 × 4 ÷ 2), which raises overhead. This is the point of Brooks's law (adding people to a late project tends to make it later <code>[V: Brooks, The Mythical Man-Month (1975)]</code>): people are not interchangeable with time.</p>\n<p><strong>Quality and the long term.</strong> Cutting quality (skipping tests, rough edges) is a loan: it saves time now and costs more later, with interest in defects, slower changes and user trust. Say which quality you are cutting, why it is safe, and when you will repay it. Accessibility, safety and honest information are not cuttable (C10). Likewise, short-term gains (a feature that lifts a number this quarter) can cost the long term (complexity, a dark pattern, a precedent). Reversibility helps: prefer the option you can undo (C02).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Naming what is fixed and what flexes turns a vague squeeze into a choice. <strong>When not to over-model.</strong> In an interview, name the fixed point, the lever you would pull and what it costs; no full model is needed.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-5-opportunity-sizing-for-prioritization\"></span>\n<h4>C15.5 Opportunity sizing for prioritization</h4>\n<p>To compare options you need a rough size for each, and you need it to carry the uncertainty. C08.6 gave a sizing ladder (reach × baseline × lift × value, with haircuts); C04 gave estimation habits. For prioritization, add two things.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected value.</strong> Multiply the size of the prize by the probability it materializes. Option A: a $2,000,000 annual benefit with a 60% chance of working, expected value $1,200,000. Option B: $5,000,000 with a 20% chance, $1,000,000. B has the bigger prize and the smaller expected value. Divide by effort for value per unit of capacity: if A takes 4 person-months and B takes 10, A returns $300,000 per person-month and B $100,000. The decision is sensitive to the probabilities, so state them as assumptions, and look for tests that would sharpen them (C15.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Bias to correct.</strong> Estimates of effort tend to be low and estimates of benefit high (the planning fallacy <code>[V: Kahneman and Tversky (1979), as summarized in reference-class forecasting sources]</code>). A defense is a reference class: how long did similar projects take, and what benefit did similar features deliver? Use it as a sanity check on your chain (<a href=\"#gl-sanity-check\">sanity check</a>, C04).</p>\n<p><strong>Ranges, not points.</strong> Give a low-base-high range for each option and see whether the ranking survives the ranges. If option A's low case beats option B's base case, the choice is easy; if the ranges overlap, the choice depends on learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Expected value per unit effort puts unlike options on one scale and makes the probability visible. <strong>When not to rely on it.</strong> When the options serve different goals (safety against growth), the unit does not convert; use criteria and constraints instead (IQ-C15-05).</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-6-assumption-mapping-risk-based-sequencing-and-the-minim\"></span>\n<h4>C15.6 Assumption mapping, risk-based sequencing and the minimum viable product</h4>\n<p>An <strong>assumption</strong> is a belief a plan depends on that has not yet been verified. Plans fail because an assumption was false and no one tested it. FW-C15-06 is the map.</p>\n<p><strong>List and place.</strong> List the assumptions a plan rests on (users want it, they will pay, the technology works, we can reach them, the law allows it, partners will cooperate). Place each on two axes: <em>how much the plan depends on it</em> (importance) and <em>how little evidence you have</em> (uncertainty). The top-right corner (important and uncertain) holds the <em>riskiest assumptions</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sequence by risk.</strong> Test the riskiest assumption first, with the cheapest test that could prove it wrong. That is &quot;risk-based sequencing&quot;: do the work that retires the most uncertainty per unit of effort before the work that is merely certain. It is the reason a team should not spend six months building polish on a feature whose central assumption (anyone wants it) is untested.</p>\n<p><strong>The minimum viable product.</strong> An <strong>MVP</strong> (minimum viable product) is the smallest thing that can test the riskiest assumption with real users. It is defined by the question it answers, not by being small or rough. Forms include: a <em>concierge</em> test (a person performs the service by hand), a <em>wizard-of-oz</em> test (the interface looks real, people operate it behind the scenes), a <em>fake door</em> (a page or button for a feature that does not exist yet, to measure demand), a <em>prototype</em> (a clickable mockup for usability), and a <em>single-feature release</em> (build one slice). Choose the form that matches the assumption: demand needs a fake door or a concierge test; usability needs a prototype; feasibility needs a technical spike.</p>\n<p><strong>Success and kill criteria in advance.</strong> Before the test, write what result means &quot;go,&quot; &quot;stop&quot; and &quot;extend,&quot; as in the experiment decision rule (C14.5). Then you cannot move the goalposts afterward. Link to the riskiest-assumption test in C06 (FW-C06-07) for the discovery version.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> It converts a big bet into a sequence of cheap questions. <strong>When not to test first.</strong> When the cost of the full build is small, the change is reversible, or the assumption is already well evidenced.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-7-saying-no-and-negotiating-scope\"></span>\n<h4>C15.7 Saying no and negotiating scope</h4>\n<p>Prioritization means refusing good ideas, and refusing well is a skill. FW-C15-07 is a script with four parts.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Acknowledge the goal or the need</strong> behind the request (&quot;You need X because customers are asking for Y&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Show the tradeoff, not just the no</strong> (&quot;To build this we'd need to drop Z, which affects W&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Offer an alternative</strong> (a smaller version, a later date, a different route that serves the need).</li>\n<li><strong>Say what would change the answer</strong> (&quot;If we learn it affects more customers, or capacity opens, we'll revisit; let's check at the end of the quarter&quot;).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Scope negotiation.</strong> When scope grows, ask the trade question: &quot;If we add this, what comes out, or what moves?&quot; Writing the roadmap as a table of what is in, what is out, and what is later makes the tradeoff visible and the conversation about priorities, not personalities. For stakeholders with authority, bring the options and your recommendation, with the cost of each, and let them decide with eyes open; do not hide the tradeoff to avoid conflict. C32 develops influence without authority.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A reasoned, kind no keeps the relationship and the credibility of the roadmap. <strong>When not to say no.</strong> When the request is cheap, important and consistent with the goal; then yes is a decision too.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-8-interview-forms-which-feature-first-what-to-cut\"></span>\n<h4>C15.8 Interview forms: which feature first; what to cut</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the answer architecture and the types: which feature first, what to cut when the deadline moves, stakeholder conflict, two teams wanting the same engineers, scoping an MVP, and choosing between a small sure thing and a big uncertain one.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-c15-9-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C15.9 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples: a scoring that flips when anchored to the goal, a weighted scorecard with a flip test for a marketplace, a deadline squeeze, an assumption map with an MVP test and a customer request that would displace the roadmap. Section 11 adds eight questions with model answers, including an Apple question on three candidates for a system release, and a Meta question and a Microsoft question.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The tradeoff table (FW-C02-03), the decision classifier (FW-C02-04), the pre-mortem (FW-C02-05), the decision memo (FW-C02-06), the riskiest-assumption test (FW-C06-07), the impact sizing ladder (FW-C08-06), the goal ladder (FW-C07-02) and the experiment decision-rule table (FW-C14-05) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-01 · The prioritization answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a set of options, a capacity or deadline, and 20 to 25 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with seven visible steps: goal and constraints, criteria, options and sizing, score and sensitivity, tradeoffs and risks, decision, what we won't do.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer clarifying questions about the goal and constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure makes the reasoning scorable and can crowd out the key insight; adapt to the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> starting with a framework; criteria not tied to a goal; no decision; no &quot;won't do.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1, and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Goal and constraints → Criteria → Options and sizing → Score and sensitivity → Tradeoffs and risks → Decision → What we won't do, and what would change it.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-02 · The framework chooser · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the kind of decision (many similar items, a release scope, time-sensitive items, strategic bets) and the data available.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a chosen framework with the reason, its main failure mode and a check that offsets it.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the data needed by the framework exists or can be estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a framework gives structure at the cost of simplification.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> one framework for everything; scoring with no data; using a score as the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the table in C15.2 and IQ-C15-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pairings: <strong>many similar items with data → RICE; fast triage → ICE; release scope for a fixed date → MoSCoW; unlike criteria → weighted scoring; time-sensitive items → cost of delay; customer expectations → Kano.</strong> Always add: source of each number, sensitivity, fit with the goal.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-03 · RICE · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> popularized by the product team at Intercom, described in a company blog post (the post is dated 5 January 2018; accounts of when RICE was first used range from 2015 to 2017) <code>[V: Intercom blog, &quot;RICE: Simple prioritization for product managers&quot; by Sean McBride; dated 5 January 2018 per the site and a content aggregator; the year RICE was first used is not established]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> for each option: reach (people or events per period), impact (a scale such as 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 3), confidence (a percentage) and effort (person-months).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a score = reach × impact × confidence ÷ effort, and a ranking.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> reach and impact are measured against the same goal; effort is comparable across options.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> quick, comparable and transparent; easy to game and to mistake for precision.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> reach not tied to the goal; impact as a guess; confidence inflated for favorites; effort underestimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 (same options, two goals).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>score = (reach × impact × confidence) ÷ effort.</strong> Add: write the goal first, define reach as people on whom the goal depends, and rerun with the least certain number changed.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-04 · The goal-to-criteria ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a goal and a list of options.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> measurable criteria derived from the goal, weights with reasons, a scored table and a flip test.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the goal is agreed; weights can be argued for.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> weighting forces explicit values and can look more objective than it is.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> generic criteria; weights tuned to a preferred option; no flip test.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2, and the example in C15.3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Goal (measurable) → What makes an option good for it → Criteria and weights → Score 1 to 5 → Flip test (change the weights) → State what is robust and what is not.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-05 · The tradeoff triangle and dials · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a plan with scope, time, quality and resources, and a change (a tighter date, new scope).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the fixed point, the lever chosen, its cost, and a plan to repay any quality borrowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> at least one of the four can flex.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> every lever has a cost; the choice is about which cost is acceptable.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> adding people late; cutting quality silently; &quot;everything is fixed.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lever</th>\n<th>Typical cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cut scope</td>\n<td>Features missing; may break the value proposition if the wrong cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Move the date</td>\n<td>Delay; dependencies; commitments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add resources</td>\n<td>Onboarding and coordination cost; works best early, for separable work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower quality</td>\n<td>Defects, rework, trust; never for safety, accessibility or honesty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-06 · The assumption map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a plan and its dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a list of assumptions placed by importance and evidence, the riskiest identified, a test or MVP for it with success and kill criteria, and the order of work.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> assumptions can be found by asking &quot;what must be true?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> testing delays the build and reduces the chance of building the wrong thing.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> listing assumptions without ranking; an MVP that tests nothing; criteria set after the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assumption</th>\n<th>Importance (H/M/L)</th>\n<th>Evidence (strong/weak/none)</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Success / kill criteria</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<p>Rule: <strong>test the high-importance, weak-evidence assumption first, with the cheapest test that could prove it wrong.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-07 · The scope-negotiation script · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a request that does not fit the plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a four-part response: acknowledge the need, show the tradeoff, offer an alternative, name what would change the answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the request has a legitimate need behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a reasoned refusal takes longer than yes or no and keeps trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a bare no; a hidden tradeoff; a yes that silently breaks the plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5, and IQ-C15-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Script: <strong>&quot;I see you need [need]. To do it we'd have to [drop/delay X], which means [consequence]. What I can do is [alternative]. If [condition], I'll revisit.&quot;</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c15-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C15-08 · The sensitivity check · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a ranked list and the estimates behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the ranking under changed values for the least certain inputs, the break-even value at which the order flips, and a verdict (robust, fragile).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you know which inputs are least certain.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> extra arithmetic for confidence in the ranking.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> varying only the favorite's numbers; no break-even value; ignoring a flip.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and the flip test in Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Find the least certain inputs → vary them across a plausible range → note where the ranking flips (the break-even) → say what you'd measure to resolve it.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"c15-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c15-the-prioritization-answer-for-a-25-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The prioritization answer, for a 25-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Goal and constraints</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The goal in measurable terms; capacity, deadline, dependencies; what is fixed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criteria</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Criteria derived from the goal, with weights and reasons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options and sizing</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Each option sized with a chain and a range; expected value if useful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Score and sensitivity</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A scored table; what happens when the least certain number changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tradeoffs and risks</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>What each choice gives up (opportunity cost); the riskiest assumption and its test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The choice, the sequence, what we won't do, what would change it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives the goal, spend the saved time on sizing and sensitivity. If you run behind, keep the goal, the criteria, a decision and the &quot;won't do.&quot; Treat questions as steering.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> Lead with the goal: &quot;I'll rank these against [goal]; my criteria are [two or three].&quot; State the least certain number and what flips: &quot;This ranking holds unless [X] is below [value].&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd build [option] first because it scores highest on [criteria] for [goal], and I'd defer [options] because [reason]. The riskiest assumption is [assumption], which I'd test with [cheap test]; I'd change the order if [condition].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-which-feature-first\"></span>\n<h4>Type: which feature first</h4>\n<p>Use the spine and the goal-to-criteria ladder. Size options, run sensitivity, decide.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-what-to-cut-when-the-deadline-moves-up\"></span>\n<h4>Type: what to cut when the deadline moves up</h4>\n<p>Use the tradeoff triangle: name the fixed point and the lever, cut scope by the goal (what is essential to the core value), list what you defer, and say what you protect (quality, accessibility).</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-stakeholder-or-customer-conflict\"></span>\n<h4>Type: stakeholder or customer conflict</h4>\n<p>Use the scope-negotiation script; bring options with costs; separate the need from the requested solution.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-two-teams-want-the-same-engineers\"></span>\n<h4>Type: two teams want the same engineers</h4>\n<p>State the criteria that apply across both (contribution to company goals, cost of delay, risk), treat non-negotiable constraints (safety, legal) as constraints and not as items to trade, and propose an allocation with a review date.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-scope-an-mvp\"></span>\n<h4>Type: scope an MVP</h4>\n<p>Name the riskiest assumption, choose the MVP form that tests it, and write success and kill criteria first.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-type-the-short-version-10-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (10 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Goal, criteria, a quick sized comparison, the decision and one risk. Protect the goal and the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What's the goal?&quot;</td>\n<td>You started with a framework</td>\n<td>State the goal and the constraint first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Where did that number come from?&quot;</td>\n<td>False precision</td>\n<td>Give the chain and a range; label as assumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if confidence is lower?&quot;</td>\n<td>Sensitivity</td>\n<td>Rerun and show whether the ranking flips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What are you giving up?&quot;</td>\n<td>Opportunity cost</td>\n<td>Name the best alternative and its value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why not do both?&quot;</td>\n<td>Capacity</td>\n<td>State the constraint and what doing both would cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;The VP wants the other one.&quot;</td>\n<td>Stakeholder conflict</td>\n<td>Use the script: need, tradeoff, alternative, trigger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Could you test first?&quot;</td>\n<td>Risk-based sequencing</td>\n<td>Name the riskiest assumption and the cheapest test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c15-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption. The arithmetic was computed. Each follows the prioritization spine (FW-C15-01) and is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-worked-example-1-the-same-five-features-two-goals-a-ranking-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — The same five features, two goals: a ranking that flips (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A consumer app has five candidate features and capacity for one or two in the quarter. A team lead scores them with RICE (FW-C15-03) and the dark-mode feature comes out on top. The company's goal for the quarter, set by leadership, is to raise new-user activation.</p>\n<p><strong>The first scoring (reach counts all users).</strong> Reach is people per quarter, impact on a 0.25 to 3 scale, confidence a percentage, effort in person-months (invented).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B Dark mode</td>\n<td>100,000</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>45,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E Onboarding checklist</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>32,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A Saved searches</td>\n<td>40,000</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>16,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D Faster checkout</td>\n<td>60,000</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>14,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C Referral program</td>\n<td>20,000</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Dark mode wins because its reach is the whole user base. But the goal is activation of <em>new</em> users.</p>\n<p><strong>Rescored against the goal.</strong> Reach becomes the new users who would encounter the feature (about 30,000 new users a quarter, fewer for features only some use); impact becomes the effect on activation specifically.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Reach (new users)</th>\n<th>Impact on activation</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>E Onboarding checklist</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>48,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D Faster checkout</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>70%</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>7,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B Dark mode</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>0.25</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>6,750</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A Saved searches</td>\n<td>8,000</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1,600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C Referral program</td>\n<td>6,000</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>750</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Dark mode drops from first to third, and the onboarding checklist, second before, leaps to first by a wide margin. Nothing about the features changed; the goal did.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity (FW-C15-08).</strong> The checklist's confidence is the least certain number (it is a new idea). At 50% instead of 80%, its score is 30,000 × 3 × 0.5 ÷ 1.5 = 30,000, still more than four times the runner-up. Its effort would have to exceed about 10 person-months (30,000 × 3 × 0.8 ÷ 7,000 ≈ 10.3) before it lost first place. The ranking is robust; the order of D and B, 7,000 against 6,750, is not, and it would be a poor use of time to argue about it.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Build the onboarding checklist. The second slot is open: D and B are close, and neither is compelling for this goal. I'd use the second slot to test the riskiest assumption in the checklist (that new users will complete it) with a prototype, not to commit to D or B. What I will not do this quarter: dark mode and the referral program, with the reason stated (not tied to activation, or low confidence and high effort) and a date to revisit.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The framework's output depended on the definition of reach and impact, which come from the goal. A ranking is only as good as the goal behind it, and the sensitivity check shows which parts are worth debating.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-worked-example-2-criteria-from-the-goal-seller-tools-in-a-ma\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Criteria from the goal: seller tools in a marketplace (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a seller tools team in an online marketplace of the kind Amazon operates. No fact about any real company is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The goal.</strong> Shorten the time it takes a new seller to make a first sale (a median of 21 days, invented). The scores below are my judgments (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria and weights (FW-C15-04).</strong> Impact on time to first sale, 40% (it is the goal); reach among new sellers, 25% (the goal concerns them); confidence, 15% (evidence for the impact); ease, 20% (capacity is limited). Each option is scored 1 to 5.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n<th>Ease</th>\n<th>Weighted score</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Guided listing wizard</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4.45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pricing suggestions</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3.30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bulk upload</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2.85</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics dashboard</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2.35</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>(Example: wizard = 0.4 × 5 + 0.25 × 5 + 0.15 × 4 + 0.2 × 3 = 2.0 + 1.25 + 0.6 + 0.6 = 4.45.)</p>\n<p><strong>Flip test.</strong> Shift the weights toward ease (impact 25%, reach 25%, confidence 15%, ease 35%): wizard 4.15, bulk upload 3.15, pricing suggestions 3.00, dashboard 2.65. And toward impact (50%, 30%, 10%, 10%): wizard 4.70, pricing suggestions 3.60, bulk upload 2.50, dashboard 2.10. The wizard is first under all three. Second place changes: pricing suggestions under impact-heavy weights, bulk upload under ease-heavy weights. So the choice of the wizard is robust and the second choice depends on how much we value ease.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision and the riskiest assumption.</strong> Build the wizard first. Pricing suggestions have the lowest confidence (2): the assumption that sellers will trust and use suggested prices is untested. Before building, run a cheap test: show suggested prices to a sample of new sellers in a concierge format and measure acceptance (C15.6). If acceptance is high, it rises to second.</p>\n<p><strong>What I would say to the team.</strong> &quot;We're ranking against time to first sale, and the wizard wins however we weigh ease against impact. The dashboard is useful for established sellers and does little for this goal, so it's not now. We'd revisit it when our goal shifts to retention of established sellers.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Criteria derived from the goal made the tradeoff explicit, and the flip test showed which part of the ranking to trust.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-worked-example-3-the-deadline-moves-up-what-do-you-cut-illus\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — The deadline moves up: what do you cut? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A team of three engineers has 8 weeks to build a feature with 10 stories (invented). Leadership moves the date to 4 weeks. The PM must respond.</p>\n<p><strong>The arithmetic (FW-C15-05).</strong> Capacity at 8 weeks: 3 × 8 = 24 person-weeks, about 2.4 per story. At 4 weeks: 12 person-weeks, enough for 5 stories.</p>\n<p><strong>Option A: add two engineers.</strong> Each new engineer is about 25% productive in week 1, 50% in week 2 and fully productive after, so over 4 weeks each delivers 0.25 + 0.5 + 1 + 1 = 2.75 person-weeks; for two, 5.5. The three existing engineers lose about 20% of two weeks to onboarding: 3 × 2 × 0.2 = 1.2. Net gain 4.3, for a capacity of 16.3 person-weeks, or about 6.8 stories. Communication paths rise from 3 to 10. Adding people gets about seven stories, not ten, and costs two engineers elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B: lower quality.</strong> Skip testing and review to save, say, 25% of effort: capacity of 12 becomes an effective 16, or about 6.7 stories, and the defects arrive later with interest. Not acceptable for the core path or for accessibility and security, and it should be a declared loan with a date to repay.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C: cut scope.</strong> Keep the 5 to 6 stories that deliver the core value, and defer the rest. The question is which. The goal is the guide: the stories without which the feature cannot deliver the user's main job stay; polish and edge-case stories go. Assume 6 of the 10 are core.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D: move the date a week.</strong> Five weeks gives 15 person-weeks, 6.25 stories, which may be enough for the 6 core stories. Worth asking whether the 4-week date is a hard constraint (a launch event, a contract) or a preference.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> First ask what is fixed: if the date is fixed, cut scope to the 6 core stories, add at most one engineer for separable work that can start on day one, protect quality on the core path, and ship the deferred stories in a follow-up about three to four weeks later with a date (four stories at 2.4 person-weeks each is 9.6 person-weeks, about three weeks for three engineers, plus review). If the date has some give, take one more week and keep scope. Present both to leadership with the cost of each: &quot;In four weeks we can deliver six of ten stories, the core flow, and the rest about four weeks later; or five weeks gets the same six with less risk. Adding people gets us to about seven stories at the cost of two engineers' other work. I'd choose scope.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What I would not do.</strong> Silently compress everything and hope; add many people to a short, interdependent project; or cut tests on the payment path.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The arithmetic made it clear that people and quality are not free substitutes for time, so the lever is scope, chosen by the goal.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-worked-example-4-assumption-map-and-an-mvp-test-group-saving\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Assumption map and an MVP test: group savings (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The idea.</strong> A savings app wants to add &quot;group savings goals,&quot; where friends save toward a shared goal. Engineering estimates four months to build (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>The assumption map (FW-C15-06).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assumption</th>\n<th>Importance</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>People want to save toward goals with friends</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Weak (a few interviews)</td>\n<td>Interviews; concierge test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invited friends will join</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Concierge test with a join-rate threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We can hold pooled money within the rules</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Legal review (cheap, early)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group goals improve retention</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Later, after launch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engineering can build it in four months</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Moderate</td>\n<td>Technical spike</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The riskiest assumption.</strong> That invited friends will join. If they do not, there is no group, and nothing else matters. It is high-importance and has no evidence. The legal question is also high-risk and cheap to test, so start it in parallel.</p>\n<p><strong>The MVP: a concierge test.</strong> Create 30 groups by hand (a person sets each up and invites friends by message), with 4 invitees per group: 120 invitees. No new software. It takes about two weeks of one PM and one operations person, against four months of engineering.</p>\n<p><strong>Success and kill criteria, written first (C14.5).</strong> Success: at least 40% of invitees join within 7 days (48 of 120). Kill: 25% or fewer (30 or fewer). Between: extend or redesign. How sure would the results be? If 48 of 120 join, the 95% interval for the true join rate is about 32% to 49%; if 30 of 120, about 18% to 33%; if 36 of 120 (30%), about 23% to 39%, which would straddle the bars and call for more data or a redesign of the invitation. The sample is small, and it answers the question of whether there is any signal, not the final rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Sequence.</strong> (1) Legal review and the concierge test now, in parallel; (2) if the test passes, a technical spike and a thin build of the smallest slice (create a group, invite, contribute); (3) retention comes later, after real use.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The test cost two weeks instead of four months, and the criteria were set before the data, so the result could actually change the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-worked-example-5-a-customer-request-that-would-displace-the-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A customer request that would displace the roadmap (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A business-software company has $50,000,000 in annual revenue (invented). Its largest customer, 8% of revenue ($4,000,000), asks for a custom feature and hints that it may leave if it does not get it. Engineering estimates 9 person-months, about $225,000 at $25,000 a person-month. Building it would displace a planned item with an expected value of $1,200,000 a year (a 60% chance of $2,000,000). Upkeep would be about 20% of the build cost a year, $45,000.</p>\n<p><strong>The arithmetic.</strong> If the customer leaves with a 25% probability when refused, the expected loss is 0.25 × $4,000,000 = $1,000,000. Building the custom feature protects that $1,000,000 but displaces $1,200,000 and adds $45,000 a year of upkeep: a net of about −$245,000 a year. The break-even is the churn probability at which protection equals cost: ($1,200,000 + $45,000) ÷ $4,000,000 = 31%. If the customer's real chance of leaving is above 31%, the custom feature pays; below, it does not. The estimate of the probability is the key assumption, and the cheapest way to improve it is to talk to the customer.</p>\n<p><strong>The approach (FW-C15-07).</strong> Do not decide on the number alone. Understand the need behind the request: what is the customer trying to accomplish, and does a general feature or a configuration serve it? If other customers have the same need, a general version has a larger payoff and spreads the cost. Then respond with the script: acknowledge the need (&quot;this matters for your operations&quot;), show the tradeoff (&quot;a custom build would delay the reporting upgrade that 40 other customers are waiting for&quot;), offer an alternative (a configurable version in the next quarter, and an interim workaround now), and name the trigger (&quot;if the workaround doesn't hold, let's meet again in six weeks with your numbers&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Precedent and concentration.</strong> Say yes to a one-off for the largest customer and the next one will ask. And revenue concentration of 8% is a risk to manage in its own right, not just a reason to comply.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Do not build the custom feature as specified. Offer the general version on a date, an interim workaround, and an executive-level conversation. Record the 31% break-even and revisit it if the customer's signals change.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The expected-value arithmetic and its break-even gave a defensible no, the script made it constructive, and the need behind the request opened better options.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;You have five features and capacity for two. Which do you build?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c15-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd use RICE: I'd estimate reach, impact, confidence and effort for each and build the two with the highest scores.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a framework named, nothing about the goal or the criteria. The scores will come from nowhere, and the answer does not say what is given up or what would change the ranking.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd ask what we're trying to achieve this quarter and what is fixed. Suppose the goal is to raise new-user activation, and capacity is two features. I'd derive the criteria from that goal: effect on activation among new users, how many new users it reaches, how sure we are, and effort.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd size each option with a reason: reach as the new users who encounter it, impact as the expected change in activation, confidence from evidence such as past tests or interviews. I'd rank them, then change the least certain number to see if the order holds. I'd pick the top two, say why, and say what I'd defer and why. I'd name the riskiest assumption in the top choice and how I'd test it cheaply.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c15-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd keep the tradeoffs explicit: building A and B means not building C, whose value I'd estimate at X, so the opportunity cost is X. I'd show the sensitivity: the top option stays first unless its effort is more than about three times my estimate, which is unlikely, while the second slot is a near tie, so I'd use it to test an assumption and not commit.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd check for second-order effects: one of the options changes the sign-up flow and could disturb existing users, so I'd ask how it affects them (the legacy-user question) and put a guardrail on it. I'd write kill criteria for the test before running it.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also say what I will not do, with a date to revisit, so stakeholders know the 'no' is a 'not now' with a reason. And I'd be ready to change the order if the goal changed: if the quarter's goal moved to retention of existing users, two of the deferred options would rise.</p>\n<p>&quot;Last, I'd treat a score as a way to organize an argument and not as the argument. What would change my mind: evidence that my reach estimate for the top option is off by more than a factor of three, or a dependency that blocks it.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c15-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States the goal and derives criteria from it</td>\n<td>Names the opportunity cost and the sensitivity of the ranking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sizes options with reasons and picks with a reason</td>\n<td>Tests the riskiest assumption cheaply, with kill criteria set first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Names what is deferred</td>\n<td>Adds a guardrail for second-order effects and a revisit date</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c15-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple ships major system software updates on a regular cadence and designs hardware, software and services together <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple: WWDC in June, public releases in September each year (iOS 26 on 15 September 2025); Apple results release describes six software platforms across all devices and its services]</code>. Third-party guides report that prioritization prompts at Apple involve choosing among candidate features for a release, and reward a clear goal, a small number of well-made things and attention to privacy and consistency across devices <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: derive criteria from a user goal, say what you would not ship, and mention effects on other devices and on developers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> In 2011 Larry Page told investors the company would put &quot;more wood behind fewer arrows,&quot; in a year when Google closed products including Google Health and PowerMeter (announced in June) and Labs (announced in July) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: CS-C15-02, Google's 20 July 2011 post and Fortune]</code> (section 10). Google has also written publicly about using objectives and key results <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Doerr, Measure What Matters (2018); Google re:Work OKR guidance]</code>. Candidates commonly report prioritization prompts at large scale <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show that you can cut as well as add, and tie priorities to a measurable objective.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon's leadership principles include Bias for Action and Frugality, and its founder has described decisions as one-way or two-way doors, with speed encouraged on the reversible ones <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles; Bezos 2015 shareholder letter]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts that probe the customer's problem first <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: classify the decision's reversibility, move fast on two-way doors and say how you would work backwards from the customer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report prioritization prompts where growth goals and safety or integrity goals compete for the same engineers <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: separate constraints (safety minimums) from tradeable goals, and propose an allocation with guardrails and a review date.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's products serve large enterprise customers with long-lived commitments and different needs <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's reasoning from public product descriptions; no source checked]</code>. Practical consequence: separate the needs of different customers and the buyer from the user, weigh strategic customers without letting one dictate the roadmap, and prefer general solutions to custom ones.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material describes informed decision-making by accountable individuals, with context over control <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report data-informed prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say who decides and on what evidence, and what you would test before committing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe serves developers who build on its products and documents API versioning to keep integrations stable <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation and engineering blog]</code>; candidates report developer-product prioritization prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: weigh the cost to developers of changes (breaking changes are expensive) alongside new capability.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c15-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Starting with a framework</td>\n<td>You say &quot;RICE&quot; before any goal</td>\n<td>State the goal and constraints first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Criteria not from the goal</td>\n<td>Generic &quot;impact and effort&quot;</td>\n<td>Derive criteria from the goal and weight them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Reach not tied to the goal</td>\n<td>Reach is all users for a new-user goal</td>\n<td>Define reach as the people the goal concerns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>False precision</td>\n<td>Scores to many digits</td>\n<td>Give ranges and a sensitivity check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>No opportunity cost</td>\n<td>Only the benefits of the chosen option</td>\n<td>Name the best alternative given up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Pros and cons with no decision</td>\n<td>&quot;It depends&quot;</td>\n<td>Decide, and say what would change it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Building before testing the riskiest assumption</td>\n<td>Six months to find out nobody wants it</td>\n<td>Map assumptions; test the riskiest cheaply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Adding people to a late project</td>\n<td>&quot;We'll add engineers&quot;</td>\n<td>Do the arithmetic; cut scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A bare no</td>\n<td>Refusal with no reason or alternative</td>\n<td>Use the four-part script</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Cutting quality silently</td>\n<td>Skipping tests to hit a date</td>\n<td>Declare the loan; protect core, safety and accessibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c15-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a> is from C02 and <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> from C03.</p>\n<p><strong>Opportunity cost</strong> (<code>MM-opportunity-cost</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-prioritization\">prioritization</a>). Every yes is a no to something else. The real price of a choice is the value of the best alternative forgone, so ask not &quot;is this worth doing?&quot; but &quot;is this worth more than the best other use of the same capacity?&quot; It is the reason the displaced item was part of the custom-feature arithmetic in Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>The outside view</strong> (<code>MM-outside-view</code>). Estimate from how similar projects actually went, not from the story of this one. Teams tend to predict their own project will go better than the typical one <code>[V: Kahneman and Tversky (1979), as summarized in reference-class forecasting sources]</code>. Ask: how long did the last three comparable projects take, and what benefit did they deliver? Use the answer to adjust effort up and benefit down.</p>\n<p><strong>The vital few</strong> (<code>MM-vital-few</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, C03). A small share of items usually accounts for most of the value, which is why the funded half of a long list can deliver most of the benefit (CS-C15-01). Look for the items with the highest value per unit effort and the largest absolute value, and be willing to leave the long tail.</p>\n<p><strong>Reversibility</strong> (<code>MM-reversibility</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, C02). Move fast on choices you can undo; slow down on those you cannot. This changes how much evidence to demand: a two-week test justifies a reversible launch, while an irreversible commitment deserves the full analysis, including a pre-mortem.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Estimates are ranges, not points; ask for a range and what would change it. Ask which options have hidden dependencies or technical risk, and which could be split into a thin first slice. Engineers often see the cheaper route to the same goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Ask what the research says about the riskiest assumption and whether a prototype test can answer it in days. Designers can often scope a smaller version that delivers most of the experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Ask for the baseline and the reach numbers you are using, and for help sizing impact with a range. Agree the success and kill criteria for any test before it runs (C14). Ask what the data cannot tell you.</li>\n<li><strong>Sales, support and stakeholders.</strong> They hold information on what customers ask for and what costs the business. Bring them the criteria and the tradeoffs early; people accept a &quot;not now&quot; they helped shape better than one handed down.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c15-checklist-before-you-commit-to-a-priority-order\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you commit to a priority order</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the goal stated in measurable terms, with the constraints?</li>\n<li>Are the criteria derived from the goal, with weights and reasons?</li>\n<li>Is each option sized with a chain and a range, and is the source of each number clear?</li>\n<li>Did I run a sensitivity check and find where the ranking flips?</li>\n<li>Did I name the opportunity cost and what we are not doing?</li>\n<li>Did I identify the riskiest assumption and a cheap test, with kill criteria set first?</li>\n<li>Did I classify reversibility, and protect quality on the core path, safety and accessibility?</li>\n<li>Do I have the four-part script ready for the stakeholders who will hear no?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c15-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c15-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C15-01 · The quarter with twelve priorities · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A product team of six engineers has 30 person-weeks of capacity for the quarter. Stakeholders have submitted twelve items, and the plan calls all twelve &quot;priorities.&quot; Last quarter the team shipped three of ten.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed capacity, a company goal to raise activation and retention, and a habit of saying yes to every request.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Each item has a value in points (a rough estimate of contribution to the goal, with stakeholders agreeing the scale) and an effort in person-weeks. Total effort is 68 person-weeks, 227% of capacity.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Effort (pw)</th>\n<th>Value per pw</th>\n<th>Cumulative effort</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>c Fix sign-up bug</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4.0</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e Notification settings</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>2.0</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b Search improvements</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a Onboarding revamp</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>1.4</td>\n<td>21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d Referral v2</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>1.4</td>\n<td>26 (over capacity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>i Help-center links</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1.3</td>\n<td>24 (after skipping d)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>g Export to CSV</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>1.25</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>f Admin dashboard</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>0.75</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>j Dark mode</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>h New integrations</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0.4</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>k Profile themes</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.4</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>l Social sharing</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>0.33</td>\n<td>not funded</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The plan asks for 68 person-weeks from a team with 30, so the team ships a few things late and the rest never.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Hold back a 20% buffer for bugs and surprises: a planning capacity of 24 person-weeks. Rank by value per person-week and fund down the list until capacity is used: c (2), e (3), b (6), a (10) is 21. The next, d, would reach 26, over capacity; the item with the next-best ratio that fits is i (3), reaching 24. Referral v2 (d) ties the onboarding revamp (a) on ratio at 1.4, but a has twice the absolute value (14 against 7), so it is funded first. The funded items are c, e, b, a and i: 24 person-weeks, 35% of the requested effort, delivering 8 + 6 + 9 + 14 + 4 = 41 of the 70 points of value, 59% of the total (the vital few, C15 mental models). The scores are rough, so sensitivity matters: the top four stay funded under reasonable changes in value; d and i are the marginal pair: d would add 3 points for 2 more person-weeks but fits only if the buffer shrinks from 6 to 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep all twelve as &quot;priorities&quot;</td>\n<td>No one is told no</td>\n<td>Most will not ship; those that do ship late; the team's credibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Fund five items to capacity with a buffer; list the rest as not now with a revisit date</td>\n<td>Value per unit capacity and predictability</td>\n<td>Seven stakeholders are told no or later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Ask for more engineers</td>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Cost and ramp-up; capacity will still fall short of 68</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs seven conversations and gives a plan that can be met and a clear reason for each decision. A feels easy and fails on delivery. C does not close a gap of this size.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Use the four-part script for each unfunded item (C15.7), share the list of what is funded and what is not, and set a checkpoint at week six to review progress and re-rank. Primary measure: the share of committed items shipped in the quarter (last quarter 30%). Guardrail: quality of shipped items (defects found in the first month). Counter measure: stakeholder satisfaction with the process, since a clear no may cost goodwill.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the team shipped all five funded items, with the buffer used on two bugs, and revisited the list at week six, adding g (Export to CSV) when a delay freed four person-weeks. Stakeholders of unfunded items were frustrated but could see the criteria; two raised new evidence and had their items re-ranked next quarter (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the buffer had been too small, the team would have dropped i and told its stakeholders early. If the value scores had been disputed, the team would have run the ranking under each stakeholder's scores and shown that the top four did not change. The lesson: a plan that fits capacity with a buffer, a stated rule for what comes next and a reason for each no beats a list of twelve priorities.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c15-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C15-02 · Google, 2011: &quot;more wood behind fewer arrows&quot; · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Google's blog post &quot;More wood behind fewer arrows&quot; by Bill Coughran, senior vice president for research and systems infrastructure (Official Google Blog, 20 July 2011), read in full (primary); Fortune's article &quot;Is this what a 'focused' Google looks like?&quot; by Verne Kopytoff (3 July 2012), read in full; and news coverage of Larry Page's remarks on Google's second-quarter 2011 earnings call and of the product closures (TechRadar, NBC Bay Area, Digital Trends, CNN/Wired, CNN Money). The earnings call itself was read through those reports. The analysis is the author's. The sources do not reveal Google's internal criteria or data. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> Larry Page took over as chief executive of Google in April 2011. On the second-quarter earnings call on 14 July 2011 he said that greater focus had been a big feature of the quarter for him: &quot;more wood behind fewer arrows.&quot; In June 2011 Google announced that it would shut down Google Health, an online service for personal health records, and Google PowerMeter, a service for monitoring home energy use. On 20 July 2011, Google announced on its official blog, under the title &quot;More wood behind fewer arrows,&quot; that it would wind down Google Labs, the site where it released early prototypes. In its statement, Google said that while it had learned a great deal from launching early prototypes there, greater focus was crucial to make the most of the opportunities ahead; many Labs products would be ended and others folded into other products. Android apps from Labs would remain on Android Market, and an update added to the post the same afternoon said that Google had no plans to change in-product experimentation channels such as Gmail Labs and Maps Labs <code>[V: Google blog post]</code>. A Fortune article of 3 July 2012 reported that Google executives had, in blog posts since the previous year, repeatedly cited focus as the rationale for closing more than 30 products or features; it named Health, PowerMeter, Slide, Buzz and Labs as closed and Knol as due to close that October, and said that Page had reorganized the company into seven groups shortly after retaking the chief executive role <code>[V: Fortune, 3 July 2012]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Google had grown a long list of products, and critics had said it had lost focus (Fortune). Engineering and management attention are limited, so each product kept has an opportunity cost in the other things that do not get built. Closing products affects the people who use them, and Labs also served as a public testing ground that some users valued.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the dates and the products named above; the stated rationale (focus); the continuation of in-product experiments in Gmail and Maps; the reorganization; the count of more than 30 closures reported in 2012. Aaron Levie, chief executive of the online file-storage company Box, told Fortune in July 2012, as Google launched a tablet, a home entertainment device, a computing service and a preview of Internet-connected glasses, that the strategy had become &quot;more wood and more arrows&quot; <code>[V: Fortune, 3 July 2012]</code>: a reminder that stated intent and later behavior can differ. A community-maintained list of retired Google products and services counted 299, according to a data-visualization page that draws on it and that reported this figure when read in October 2026 <code>[V: secondary]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The problem is an allocation problem: too many projects for the capacity, so each gets too little. As an observable problem: &quot;Many products, each with a small team and uncertain traction, spread attention thin and made it unclear which mattered most.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Through this chapter's frameworks, the case shows prioritization by <em>cutting</em>. The opportunity cost of a product is the best use of the people and attention it consumes (C15.1), and the decision to close Health, PowerMeter and Labs is a statement that other uses are worth more. The stated rule, fewer and bigger bets, is a criterion derived from a goal: focus on areas the company judged central (on the earnings call Page described three groups of products: the core advertising business, high-usage consumer products such as Android, Chrome and YouTube, and new products such as Google+ and Local <code>[V: TechRadar report of the call]</code>; Fortune reports the seven-group reorganization, with executives over advertising, social networking, Android, Chrome, YouTube and local mobile commerce <code>[V: Fortune]</code>). The way it was done shows the script (FW-C15-07) at company scale: give the reason (focus), name what continues (in-product experiments, some Labs products folded in), and offer an alternative where there is one.</p>\n<p>The costs are the legacy-user and trust costs from C08: people who relied on a product lose it, and a company known for closing products may find users slower to adopt the next one <code>[inference]</code>. The critic's remark shows the second risk, that a stated priority is only as good as the follow-through. What the sources do not show is the quantitative basis: which products had what traction, what the freed teams built instead, and whether the strategy paid. So the case supports the <em>method</em> (cut to fund focus, say why, protect what matters) and not a claim that the specific cuts were right.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a leadership team could have faced; not a record of Google's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep all products and add resources</td>\n<td>Breadth; no one is told no</td>\n<td>Attention stays spread; cost grows; the largest bets are not larger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Close low-traction products, redeploy teams to central bets, announce the reasoning</td>\n<td>Focus and clarity</td>\n<td>Users of closed products; reputational cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Freeze low-traction products in maintenance mode</td>\n<td>Avoids closure backlash</td>\n<td>Ongoing cost and attention; products linger</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B recovers capacity and states priorities clearly, at the cost of disappointing users and critics. A is easy and keeps the problem. C is a compromise that leaves much of the cost in place.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Google chose B in a series of announcements in June and July 2011 and afterwards, together with a reorganization.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: closures of Health, PowerMeter and Labs, and, according to Fortune in July 2012, more than 30 products and features closed with focus cited as the rationale, with the continuation of in-product experimentation <code>[V: Fortune; Google blog post]</code>. Whether it improved Google's results is not established in the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the closures been announced with no reason, users would have read them as arbitrary, and the focus message would have been lost. Had the freed capacity gone to new scattered projects, the critic's quip would have been true. A reader should note that a famous decision looks clearer in hindsight than it was. The method to carry away: treat what you stop as a prioritization decision with an opportunity-cost rationale, communicate the reason and what continues, protect the people affected where you can, and check later that the freed capacity went where you said it would.</p>\n<span id=\"c15-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eight questions: two Beginner, three Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure is an assumption; the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the prioritization spine (FW-C15-01). Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c15-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C15-01 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you prioritize features?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I use RICE&quot; (or another framework), with nothing more.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, derives criteria, sizes options and decides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats frameworks as tools, runs a sensitivity check, names the opportunity cost and the riskiest assumption, and says how stakeholders are brought along.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I start with the goal, because the same list ranks differently under different goals. I ask what we are trying to move this quarter and what is fixed: capacity, the date, dependencies. Then I derive criteria from the goal: who must be affected, by how much on the goal metric, how sure we are, and what it costs. If the goal is new-user activation, reach means new users, not everyone.</p>\n<p>Then I size each option with a reason and a range, and I use a framework such as RICE only to organize the numbers: reach, impact, confidence, effort. I'd say where each number comes from and what the least certain one is. I rerun the ranking with that number changed; if the top option holds, I trust it, and if two options swap, I know that is where to spend discussion or a small test.</p>\n<p>I name the opportunity cost: choosing A means not building B, and I say what B was worth. I look for the riskiest assumption in the winner and the cheapest way to test it before a full build. I decide, say what we are not doing and when we'd revisit it, and I take the reasoning to stakeholders so a &quot;not now&quot; is understood, not imposed.</p>\n<p>The mistake I'd avoid is letting a score be the decision. A framework gives a way to talk about the options; the decision is a judgment about the goal, the evidence and the tradeoffs.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n";
